<<~ ahu #/cold-start>>

!! Cold start ~ what a cold session reads, runs and obeys

!!! Read, in this order
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot">>, the boot seed; then this carrier, top to bottom.
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths">> before the first commit: which session holds which ground, and the commit isolation that guards hands inside one session.
# <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/handoff">> before the first test or wire: the red-first procedure in eight moves, the ladder for wiring a module that stands unreached, the traps this tree paid for, and the grammar watch before any grammar, frame or ingest work.
# Bring every ruling #/open names to the operator before a lane builds against it.

!!! Instruments ~ run each fresh before trusting a reading
* ''Branch'': `feature/lararium-node-5`. `git rev-list --count origin/feature/lararium-node-5..HEAD` reads the unpushed commits. The operator pushes, and pushes a head whose shard run read green. Never a lane's to move, commit, stash or revert: `.worktrees/`, `.claude/worktrees/`, the `mempalace` submodule pointer, and the `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` pointer, which the operator pins at the released grammar while the grammar's own head stands ahead of that pin.
* ''Unit suites'': each package's own, under a scratch `LAR_ROOT` with `LAR_BAGS` unset; `node --test tools/*.test.mjs` for the tools. An import failure reads as zero tests, so read the file count beside the test count. `pnpm -r test` skips the root `tests/` directory by design.
* ''Typecheck'': `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`. Typecheck reads source and the binary reads `dist`, so run the built binary after any change that crosses packages.
* ''Witness fleet'': `bash tools/witness-all.sh`, the whole fleet, one run at a time; a hand-picked subset reads greener than the tree. It holds out the witnesses its own `HELD_OUT` table names (the docker, browser and tree-clean ones). Capture its exit code directly, never through a pipe; `git status --porcelain` reads identical before and after a sound run.
* ''E2E'': every set rides the CI shards (`tools/e2e-shards.mjs`, `.github/workflows/e2e-shards.yml`; `E2E_SETS` names the sets and the merge gate checks each set's union). `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` pushes HEAD to a throwaway `ci/<lane>` branch. A local run of one file proves that file alone.
* ''CI'' (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`): `gh run list --branch feature/lararium-node-5` names the run for the pushed head; a later commit waits for the next push. `held-out.yml` runs weekly or by hand, and a hosted run of it that reads green stays owed.
* ''The docker mesh'' (by hand): `crossing-witness`; `node tools/mesh-pins.mjs --up` stands the mesh pinned; `herm-mesh-witness` carries `node/alpha` across three hops; `mesh-scenarios nexus`. The mesh publishes `MESH_PORT_*` on loopback, every node service requires `LARES_HARNESS_IMAGE`, and `tools/harness-image.sh` checks its digest. A hearth whose log carries a `[lararium] fatal:` line reads as not stood.
* ''Known environmental reds'': `lararium-tw5` `tests/syncer-back-parity.e2e.test.ts` (Chromium legs) and `tests/meme-routes.e2e.test.ts` fail where no browser or server socket stands; the browser suite reads `no tests` under a `HOME` that holds no Playwright Chromium.
* ''The operator's live `~/.lares` vessel'' holds an earlier shape that this build refuses. Never stand it here; it takes the fresh start, by hand (#/fresh-start).

!!! Steps
# `export LAR_ROOT=/tmp/lar-scratch-<you>` before any in-tree boot or any `lares` command that touches the store. `LAR_ROOT` moves the store, the bags root and every projection mirror root; `LAR_BAGS` alone moves only the bags root, and the projection still writes the tracked `bags/` and `wikis/`. A staged e2e vessel takes its own scratch `LAR_ROOT` and no global `LAR_BAGS`. One `lares meme normalize|check` reads files and needs only a scratch `LAR_BAGS`.
# `LAR_ROOT` leaves these homes where they stand: `~/.mempalace` (the destructive `mempalace repave` among its readers), `~/.venv`, `~/.local/bin`, and the harness homes `~/.claude`, `~/.codex`, `~/.copilot`. A `vessel stand --codex` under a scratch root still writes the operator's real harness home.
# Build: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` only where a lead approved an install, then `pnpm -r build`, then `pnpm --filter @lararium/tw5 build:tw5-vendor` (the TW5 core blob that `head-parity` and the plugin smoke need), then `node tools/stamp-build.mjs`. A fresh `git worktree` holds empty submodules; populate them before building there.
# ''A founding wants a human route on a TTY.'' With no TTY it refuses `CredentialTtyRefusal`, and no flag or environment variable carries a credential. A harness founds through the test door: `tools/test-scratch-marker.mjs` stages the root, `tools/seed-test-custody.mjs` names the published test route, then `lares vessel found --skip-build`; `tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs` opens a marked scratch root. An agent may read vessel state and hand the operator the next founding command as text; the operator performs each founding and custody act.
# ''Every boot rests at the floor.'' A verb that needs sovereign state refuses at a standing floor and names `lares vault unlock`. A harness that reads the first `gate key:` line of a log reads the carriage relay's key where a relay stands.
# ''The container'': `tools/lares-container.sh` offers `up`, `down`, `found` and `logs` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor">>). ''The browser surface'': `tools/browser-ci.sh` over `docker-compose.browser-ci.yml`, one project and image tag per builder (`BROWSER_CI_PROJECT`, `BROWSER_CI_IMAGE`).
# The tw5 plugin bundle (`packages/lararium-tw5/plugins/lares-memetic-wikitext.json`, `plugins/standalone/*`, `src/plugin-tiddler.generated.ts`) rides in git and must equal a fresh build: rebuild after any tw5 source or tiddler change and commit it with the change. CI's `build-freshness` job checks it.
# `lares-cli` `tests/pour-repave.test.ts` needs the dev-root marker (`LAR_DEV_REPO_ROOT=1` or the ignored `lar-dev-root.json`).
# Crew shape, by standing grant: one Opus lead drives two Luna builders and one Sol reviewer, five rounds per set at most; builders commit on the feature branch with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>`.

!!! The standing law that bites ~ read the carrier before arguing with it
* ''The frame stands as canon'': DOCTYPE, the SOH bearing, STX, `toml meta` first in the body, the body, ETX with its `ni:///sha-256;…` check, EOT. One span per carrier; a spelling outside the frame grammar tears the frame (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing">>).
* ''The frame's `?` and the gate's unechoed key stay distinct.'' `from="?"` declares an unresolved bearing at the grammar layer; the gate withholds a known key at the transport layer. Their one shared law reads at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/system-pattern-integrities#/threshold-pattern-integrities">>.
* ''Ingest decides from a parse and a verdict.'' The deserializer stands as the pure meme-to-tiddlers function; `verdict()` (Match · Stale · Absent · Torn · Bare) lives in `@lararium/memetic-frame`; checks live in `carrier-check.ts`, repairs in normalize, policy at each door. A stale check on a human edit warns; a torn frame refuses; text with no frame reads as bare data, never a meme.
* ''Never refuse on the stock syncer path''; surface on the alert rail (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-back-parity">>).
* ''The quoteblock floor fences at two grains'': the whole chunk inside the deserializer, and a child's body in place at the child gate (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor">>).
* ''An admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors.'' The gate reads the presented admit against the carriage board's counted revokes and the kapae antigen, and folds no allow roster (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine">>).
* ''No global now'' and ''the fence'': #/rulings.
* ''One holder per store, one Lararium per human'': a second holder meets `StoreHeld` across containers and namespaces (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor">>).
* ''Fragments'': every `lar:` fragment roots at `#/…`, one raw `#`, `/` nests, `$` reserved (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext">>).
* ''Grammar lives in the tiddlers'' (`packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers/sigil-*.tid` generate `grammar-table.generated.ts`); sigil heads stay core ASCII; `aka` pins as information, `kanawai` pins as binding, `kahea` transcludes live.
* ''Early alpha'': no back-compat, no shims, no legacy recovery; a stale thing aligns to the current frame and grammar, and the fence (#/rulings) ends that line (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wild-bytes">>). Derive, never enumerate. One function, one relation.
* ''A registry compiled about subjects refuses at any scope'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>).
* ''A relation's evidence keeps its type'' and ''a worker keeps its own storage scope'': <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/causal-island#/selected-contracts">>.
* ''Shared tree'': never `git stash`; commit only your paths; never `--no-verify`; a spirit spawns no helper and never juggles another hand's uncommitted hunks; read an exit code directly; prove a zero with a known positive (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/law">>).
* ''The node side routes nothing into the shore's files'' while a shore lane holds them: `packages/lararium-browser/**`, `packages/lararium-web/**`, `tw5-host-bridge.ts`, `tw5-projection.ts`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rulings>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The live test and the fence
* ''The live test runs with TWO operators on two machines from its first day'': the operator and the operator's daughter, in one house. The face-switch act, the cabal plane, the custody seal and the engine fold ALL land before it.
* ''The fence.'' The second operator's SEAT beside the first at the first Nexus's founding ENDS the alpha line. Every destroy-class or shape-changing act lands BEFORE it: the engine re-mint and the genesis re-bake; the carrier and key-class changes; the Stable re-found (AID, charter, seat, keyring); every signed-shape change; every load-bearing rename. After it, data deserves a migration path.
* ''A working Lararium comes first'': Live Wiki Browser -><- Live Node, under manual QA on one machine. Sensorium work waits behind that milestone.
* ''A founding road carries its own succession.'' A reconstitution kit lets another competent operator stand a node and read its grammar; a rite held in one operator's head alone carries no succession.
* ''Face plurality stands as a day-one requirement.'' Each of the two humans wears at least three faces, each with a public Handle, on the first Nexus.

!!! The house as a container
* ''All CODE runs dockerized''; the host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment (files and bags, session capture, the editor and CLI entry, custody).
* ''ONE Lararium per HUMAN, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel (`PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one`); the recorded face-switch act carries plurality. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face.
* ''A SESSION wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator-ruled; quote license: //"a human might want a tab open as one Persona, and a tab open as another Persona... same vessel. Identity as relation."// and //"a CLI session wears its own face too … those act as ephemeral UX"//). A vessel holds its roots and one store. A session — a browser tab, a CLI run — contracts in as ONE persona, and several sessions may live at once over one vessel, each wearing its own. Every act carries exactly one face. Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire: each session proves itself under its own face-scoped key, never under a key the vessel shares across them. The vessel-wide parts (custody, the daemon island) serve every session and let none read another's planes.
* ''Local-first first.'' Cloud "backup larariums" under other humans' delegated keys stand DEFERRED until the operator can deploy to a cloud surface. Nothing in the container or the face-switch work may foreclose a keyless ciphertext carrier later.
* ''The boundary'' (settled by the house under those rulings): one multi-arch image; `/workspace` mounts bags and wikis, `/state` mounts vessel homes and custody, never an image layer; the local socket stays INSIDE the container and the host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`; the app never receives the docker socket; mDNS offers unauthoritative LAN candidates and a pinned admission alone grants authority; DNS-01 wildcard TLS supplies browser secure contexts; a release manifest binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs.

!!! No global now
* ''A wall clock never decides whether an act runs.'' Abandonment, revocation and expiry ride causal events; a timer serves only its holder's own liveness, and a device clock may sit under an adversary's hand. Web2.5 timers (TLS acceptance, OAuth tokens) sit at the edge of the causal island.
* ''Lapse rides a causal fence'': an epoch roll, a burn marker, or causal supersession at a verified relation fence. A replica missing that history reports an incomplete view, never "expired"; quiet never means lapse.

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Lararium holds a charter.'' One operator's charter reads as a SEED and two operators' seats in one charter read as a NEXUS (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). A household seed never grows into a Nexus; a Nexus founds fresh at threshold two of two. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups. A Nexus grows by adding seats through its own rolls.
* ''A household seed serves its holder alone.'' A one-operator seed gives durable use without recruiting a mesh; useful local standing precedes every wider relation.
* ''Guests walk the household seed.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents plus the public and infrastructure shelf.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its household seed, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Planes ~ cabal, realm, Nexus
* ''CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS names the federated hardware mesh.'' The Nexus `@crossroads` board serves public-infrastructure docs and the Kahu Cabal keeps them. An operator's offering to a book club belongs in that club's realm, signed by the face worn there.
* ''Kahu drafts in the realm the charter derives''; publishing to `@crossroads` stands as a separate, recorded promotion act. A second Kahu realm gets made only for a genuinely different relation.
* ''Promotion approves the EXACT BYTES'' (hash, purpose, surface) under a threshold the charter sets, with a higher bar for charter, admission and trust-root changes. A CID, a clerk's edit or an active persona never stands in for collective approval.
* ''Steward signing.'' A scoped face signs acts that belong to one person; a transparent quorum Handle signs acts the collective owns; the root never appears publicly. A human wears MANY faces and many public Handles, each in many cabals, and signs a steward edge with the face or Handle worn IN THAT CABAL.
* ''Oracle answer'': one public answer per Nexus, signed by that Nexus's Kahu steward; a herm carries and presents it and signs nothing public. The engine floor rides as one shared content-addressed bake, and each answer names its floor CID (operator ruling).
* ''Herm tending'': tending may reach the herm's key; the house accepts that reach and names it (operator ruling).
* ''Introduction and admission'': a user introduces; an operator admits; the answer consumes the introduction (operator ruling).
* ''Names and counters'': the keyring epoch name joins (Nexus AID, `epochCid`); a counter orders one writer's own emissions only. The convergence secret stays per Nexus, and the face lives at the session capability (operator ruling).
* ''Kahu acts fall into four chartered classes'': ordinary (1-of-1); high-impact — charter, admission, trust root (3-of-5, or 2-of-3 for a small body); emergency (2-of-3, a narrow pre-authorized power reviewed by a later causal act); revocation (the threshold of the original act). A challenge appends to the record and may pause LOCAL reliance; it never halts the protocol. Admission of a Kahu or a Lamplighter always takes the seat quorum, and no ordinary single-seat class covers it; kapae and rolls take the quorum too; the user road rides its own bound (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>).
* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
* ''A cancel crossing a herm yields three receipts'': the caller's abandonment, the relay's delivery (transit only), the vessel's outcome. A caller timeout never counts as a cancel; eviction answers `retention-gap`.
* ''One face at a time names WHO ANSWERS for an act.'' The switch law reads in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/one-face`.
* ''A session wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling).

!!! Custody ~ the Vessel KEK
* One Vessel KEK (VK), minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of VK. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers a VK rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only; a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal.
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': VK and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter. The built allowance carries the pre-answered dial of the user road's three; the operator's answer to a user's introduction stands owed, and until it lands a walker's token earns standing with no answer (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree; the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so the isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The first ceremony, and the acts around it (operator ruling)
* ''Guests'': the first ceremony runs with the host's own invites only. Guest introductions stay off until the hosted road's re-cut lands; the hosting act's new bytes still land before the fence.
* ''Reach on the day'': LAN only. The public domain's Handle table comes later.
* ''At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.''
* ''An operator's own herm'': an operator contracts their own herm with one signature, scoped to one place and one Nexus, and it falls with a kapae of the operator's admit.
* ''The hosting key'': the operator's seat signs ONE delegation to a hosting key; that key signs each hosting act and seeds each epoch. A session wearing any other face signs nothing for the hearth.
* ''The cold-key card'': the house format — 64 hex plus a checksum, in groups of four — printing its purpose, chair label, generation and a short charter fingerprint.
* ''The DNS credential'': the registrar's credential rests as two files on the first operator's always-on node, outside both mounts, read by the one-shot issuer. That node issues one wildcard per machine, and the second machine receives its certificate by hand.
* ''A browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root''; the mint refuses without one.
* ''No guardians on the day'': each face starts one-of-one on its own paper, and enrolment comes later by a rotation on the face's own log.
* ''Shore saves, as built'': a rename onto a standing title proceeds as an ordinary write at field grain; a tombstone moved into a bag that lacks the title refuses unless the hand asks to shadow.
* ''A seat gets ADDED, never replaced.'' The long-span act adds a holder to BOTH cabals — a Kahu seat and a Lamplighter contract — and the founders keep their chairs; one founding Kahu may hold seats across many Nexuses. The road proves the roll that adds a seat, and the new holder's carriage contract, first. A passed chair stays a possible act. A Kahu who works far from the other seats needs a co-signature for every quorum act, so the partial-and-cosign door carries a partial act across a relay and back with no clock.
* ''Per-Nexus identifiers'': an admit names a per-Nexus identifier, a random leaf no other Nexus can link. The face's lineage may re-issue it by a signed hand-over the gate verifies, and per-Nexus keys mint at random, never from the persona seed.
* ''Every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>). A bounded ember stays design input: it raises to SERVING alone, never signs or admits, runs on a count and never a clock, needs the device's own unlock plus a second signal, records each raise, and any other device of the human removes it.

!!! The fresh start
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! Decisions ~ what still shapes unbuilt work, and why

The hosted walk's own mechanics (the token, the grant, the spent-set, the hosting doc) read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`. The decisions below constrain a slice
still on the road.

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every gate instance (`/ws`, `/oracle`, the membership relay) sorts before `lar:auth-ok`, so posture reads once, at admission, and never again at share time. Two readers of one posture drift. R-WIRE keeps it: one gate per (Nexus, route).
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gate-knock`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key (R-WIRE) — not an interim.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case. The face-switch act inherits it.
* ''A cliff takes a funnel, then one assembled commit.'' Where one shape change crosses three packages, the fixtures funnel through one helper first; narrow builders each write one patch against a fixed contract; one lander proves them together in a scratch worktree and commits once, so the main tree never holds half a flip. Three cliffs stand on the road: the charter flip, the Nexus rename and the socket cutover.
* ''Who signs changes before how the next keys get committed.'' The seats take their own keys under the standing charter shape; the per-seat cold digests follow in the flip. Between those two commits a seat's next key rests on the same disk as its current one, so pre-rotation protects nothing there, and no vessel that matters gets founded in that span.
* ''The socket's handshake changes once.'' R-WIRE and the face proof build as ONE wire train: the signed admit, then the fleet-join door, then one socket cutover (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof">>). The tree between those steps reads no earlier shape; it stands as a build order, never a compatibility mode.
* ''A pure library names its caller.'' A module built ahead of its consumer stays out of the package barrel until the commit that consumes it. Until a production path calls it, a module stands specified in code, never built.
* ''`vessel found` keeps a charterless road.'' The Stable founding act becomes the HEARTH's rite; a herm still founds as a place with no face, and a joiner still founds through an admit.
* ''An earlier charter shape refuses by name''; today it reads torn, and torn reads PRIVATE.
* ''The carriage verifier holds the root'': it checks the epoch, the signature AND a subject root it holds locally, and refuses by name without that root.
* ''Guardian placement'' reads as advice the house names at enrolment, never a refusal. ''QR pairing'' shows a check string on both screens after the channel opens.
* ''The floor vessel key drops its next-key commitment'', inside the one identity train. It changes the vessel's signed inception bytes; the operator may overturn it.
* ''A long-silent fleet device'' (lean, to ONE FACE's plan): each certificate presentation carries the frontier of the withdrawal history its presenter saw; a gate holding a later frontier asks the device to catch up first, and until then the device reads `unconfirmed` and acts on nothing shared. A gate that learned nothing newer may accept. No clock.
* ''The strangers' dial drops the NEWEST arrival'' when its slots fill; a slot frees by the operator's answer or a roll.
* ''Every browser leaf counts as evictable'': `storage.persist()` refuses in a plain tab, so no leaf holds the only copy of anything, and no passkey slot stands as the only way in (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026">>).
* ''A restarted always-on node serves no hosted phone until a human unlocks.'' The resolver and the terminator keep answering, so the page loads and the sync waits; the page says so in one line, until the ember lands.
* ''A crossing gets recorded, never interior life.'' Only an authority, vessel, federation or externally meaningful crossing becomes distributed canon (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/mandate-activity-provenance">>).
* ''A package names no vessel.'' Every entity starts at the herm floor; a seated persona lights the Lararium stack over it. Only a lararium lights a Pronaos, and a herm mirrors a house's published bytes by CID (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-placement">>).
* ''Deferred by the house'': realm feeding (two carriers disagree on max over per-writer slots against a blank merge; it waits for the first-realm arc), community exit, and funding, where payment may buy capacity and never standing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-road>>

!! The road ~ what stays unbuilt, in the order a builder walks it

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon. ''The fence''
(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.

''The order'': GROUND → SUBSTRATE → HARBOR → KAHU PLANE → ONE FACE → TWO HEARTHS. The re-found's train
rides inside it — R-ENGINE's fold and R-CUSTODY's seal in SUBSTRATE; R-CHARTER → R-WIRE, then R-KAHU,
R-ENGINE's offers and R-EMBER, in the KAHU PLANE. Where the train's older order (the engine after the wire)
and the six paths disagree, operator-ruled order governs: the engine fold and the custody
seal land early, and the container's tail lands before the charter train.

!!! 1 · GROUND ~ the residue (no identity bytes; waits on nothing; rides beside SUBSTRATE on disjoint files)
* ''Outcome-doc rotation'', so the receipt ring bounds bytes as well as count.
* ''The herm route for cancel and receipts'': the three receipts of #/rulings, mesh → node → CLI.
* ''`KeyhiveIdentitySlot` production wiring.''
* ''The evergreen sweep and the quiet doors'', itemized in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff#/wounds`.
* ''Measures'': the full-corpus per-verb flush re-measure; pack-PIN recursion; a production `assertBodyIndexTier` caller.
* ''Canon direction the build owes'': canon crosses hearth → leaf; a leaf never writes the hearth's canon documents, and edits land in the working layer. The admitted leaf-side mount flag stops mount-path writes, while Repo sync carries those documents in both directions.
* ''Clockless shore law the build owes'': no wall clock decides; the binding record carries a readerless `minted-on` stamp that leaves (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/resolve-binding.ts`).
* ''A binding lands after its document rests'': a slot mint records the binding only on the vessel store's acknowledgment (the rest door, `doc:rest` / `doc:rested`); a mint no binding names stays listed (`lares vessel unbound`), and nothing removes one.

!!! 2 · SUBSTRATE ~ two SOLO relays, the fold first, then the seal
Each runs one spirit per commit, and every commit leaves HEAD building. No other node lane runs beside the
seal: it owns the compose hunk.

''R-ENGINE, the fold.''
* The R-ENGINE fold target dependency line, in fixed order: automerge-repo and its four listed adapters at alpha.5 → `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.1 → `@keyhive/keyhive` 0.3.0-alpha.1; no Subduction or Onomancy dependency → the genesis re-bake (`GENESIS_ENGINE_CID`, every fixture holding the hearth true-name, the untracked CAS in the same commit) → the fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image, with a pushed shard run reading green. The manifests hold all three dependency steps (`packages/lararium-node/package.json`, `packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json`); a Keyhive Archive and a cap event carry no format tag, so bytes one Keyhive version wrote refuse under another and a version move re-founds every vessel. The fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image stands owed.
* TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.

''R-CUSTODY, the seal'' (P3-SEAL).
* ''What stands sealed'': the custody root and the `identity-anchors` family: the keyhive archive, each face's own veil archive (`veil-archive-h<N>.bin`), each face's anchors and the anchor roster. A founding seals them from its first write and hands out every keyhive Archive it stood before the provider leaves; the veil derives from the sealed custody root; a seat (`custody-seat.ts`) carries one holder per identity home, and each row reads whole or refuses by name, never empty. A creator that stands again restores from its Archive: cap events carry membership and never a creator's key agreement state. A stand mints one contact card and its Archive leaves after that mint, so the Archive lineage opens every prekey the identity ever presented. A browser vessel keeps the Archives its founding and its admit hand out in its keystore beside the device seed, in the clear, until browser custody wraps both (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`).
* ''What it owes'': every other `hot` and `cold` row of the carrier table (`vault-carriers.ts`) still rests outside the VK — its writer's bytes pass through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and its reader through the sealed open. One commit per writer family, derived from the table's writer column, never from a hand list.
* ''The keyring law the build owes'': name an epoch by (Nexus AID, `epochCid`); let a counter order one writer's own emissions only; keep the convergence secret per Nexus and the face at the session capability. Mint only at the seat. Join delivery by scoped key and refuse different bytes under one name. Keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence; pass hot bytes through the sealed writer (operator ruling).
* ''The Nexus keyring debt'': the store keys entries by numeric epoch, mints at boot, overwrites a same-epoch delivery, and writes clear JSON (`packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-convergence-secret-store.ts:35-60,72-81,100-116`).
* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no VK and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened VK in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The passphrase lifecycle refuses a VK-sealed carrier by name, and the settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no VK, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed row it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route.
* ''Facts to plan around'': the hosting spend log APPENDS, and a whole-file sealed writer cannot, so it moves to one sealed file per spend; the CLI-side writers move behind node functions, so no CLI file writes a custody row.
* ''Last commit, the retirement'': `vault seal|rotate|repair|passphrase` and `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE` (with its `_NEW` twin in `tools/contract-witness.sh`) leave with the passphrase lifecycle — never sooner, since they hold the only seal the share carriers carry; the bare-row rotation preflight leaves, and the rotation's own census runs BEFORE the unbind, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound; `vault unbind --rotate` then completes on a real founded vessel, where it refuses by name until that day.
* The census DERIVES from the carrier table, and a witness refuses any 0600 file or seal outside it. The enumerable carriage rows and the leaf-testability bound close at rest here.

!!! 3 · HARBOR ~ the container's tail
* ''A persona door before `up`'': `tools/lares-container.sh` routes CLI acts through the offline door before `up`; after a successful act in a standing vessel the host shim reads the standing verdict and restarts the vessel on `restand`. The verdict reads a LIT face alone, so a `persona wear` that changes the worn face on a standing vessel waits on ONE FACE.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': the module and wiring stand off by default; `LAR_LAN_DISCOVERY=announce` enables announcements without keys. A real-LAN run and the network mode remain owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/lan-discovery`).
* ''Household HTTPS'': the compose `https` profile supplies a terminator and a resolver slot, the `issuer` profile supplies a one-shot DNS-01 issuer that reads the registrar's credential from two owner-read files outside both mounts (`docker-compose.surface.yml`; `tools/household-https-witness.sh` walks issuance against a test authority), and `deviceAdmitSyncUrl` derives relay reach from `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. The operator supplies the domain and the credential files. Owed: the reach face's code half (a mismatch logged once, a loud boot notice where no setting stands, a forged-header test), per-name resolver records, the renewal verb with its reload, and a first live issuance. No device takes an install (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/household-https`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. A whole `mesh-scenarios all` run on that shape stays owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
* ''The release manifest'': `tools/release-manifest.mjs` emits the record shape; a real multi-arch index, its push and signing remain owed.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': many cabal realms per Nexus with selected-realm routing, where one charter-derived realm plane stands today. A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root. The docker `meme` scenario fails at "B edits, promotes" — the steward gets named by a per-Nexus LEAF key while the realm-bag co-sign looks for the persona ROOT — and it proves the end-state signer, never a root workaround and never a leaf fallback.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'': the four chartered classes and exact-bytes promotion of #/rulings; chartered Kahu-cabal admission as its own authority act, where a vouch alone admits nobody.

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train'' (waits on SUBSTRATE whole).
* ''The Stable founding act'': one interactive act under one passphrase (the VK's first slot) yields the place, persona zero, a PRIVATE seed charter (one seat, t=1, the seat's own key and its cold next digest) and a keyring named by (Nexus AID, `epochCid`), with a commitment to the secret inside the seat-authored epoch act; mint only at the seat, never at boot (operator ruling).
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>).
* ''Charter shape'': per-seat pre-rotation (`nextKeyDigests`, `nextThreshold`) replacing the single next-key commit; the cadence inception cited once by the founding act; the seal-roll successor takes the name `charter-seat-roll`; `epoch0-<sha>` names become `nexus-<sha>`; `genesisSealEpochCid` deleted; the seat arms its cold next key at Stable founding (operator ruling).
* ''Re-scoped keys'': the chairs re-key to their own SEAT keys, apart from persona roots and per-Nexus dialing leaves (a root-signing seat breaches Q1-strict; a leaf-signing seat puts a warm dialing key in the quorum); the device edge and the dyad scope commit to the Nexus AID; the sentinel (the MeshCabal keyhive identity) re-keys.
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the keyring joins delivery by scoped key and refuses different bytes under one name; one fleet-join door replaces `device-admit` and `persona admit`; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.
* The staging harness founds through the one act inside the train, because the e2e set reads red from the first charter-byte change until the founding lands.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
* A per-Nexus wire key derived from the sealed custody root and the Nexus AID; one gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly.
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock becomes the IK pre-message. It retires the `lar:challenge` frame.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''Owed (a), the dialer's name rides the clear'': the dialer's ContactCard crosses in cleartext in `lar:auth` over plain `ws://` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/auth-wire.ts`, `LarAuthMsg`) — a live THR_2 leak that Noise IK's encrypted first message cures, the dialer's static riding inside message one instead.
* ''The admit signature the build owes'': the canon-document list and gate key enter the signed bytes; `device-admit.ts` carries them in `carried`, beside the signed `payload` (`packages/lararium-node/src/commands/device-admit.ts`). The browser's first proof rides `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`).
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC of the public gate key (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-knock.ts`, `knockSegment`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

''Behind the train, each waiting on R-CHARTER''.
* ''R-KAHU'': S1, a quorum whose seats live on different vessels, and L1, the realm root map from the co-signed `keptBy`. Needs only the seat shape.
* ''R-ENGINE, the offers'': the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''R-EMBER'': a household's first ember stands on its second device, which needs the fleet-join door; the slot tree carries passphrase and paper pins today, and this slice adds the ember pin kind.

!!! 5 · ONE FACE ~ the recorded switch act (waits on the signer and the realm shapes)
* The slice builds `session-contract.mem` in its build order and `face-proof.mem`. A session wears the face; the vessel wears none. The switch acts on ONE session and closes and re-dials only that session's links under the incoming face's scoped proof.
* ''Working-layer law the build owes'': one working document per (face, wiki, slot kind); its address derives from a secret only that face's vessels hold. The working layer follows its wiki across recipe recomposition. A leaf with an admit and no seat writes nothing to face wikis until join completion. Vessel removal leaves the address standing; the gate and document membership refuse the removed vessel (operator ruling).
* A wiki names one face's composition over bags; sharing happens at the bag (operator ruling).
* A personal Handle never rides without its owning face; one current personal Handle per PersonaGroup; a collective quorum Handle stays separate.
* ''Every hand opens a session'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>): a tab, a CLI run, the disk watcher and a spirit's MCP seat differ by capability profile and never by key; MCP and the CLI stand as two doors onto one contract. The slice owes the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, and door parity at the session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as its system identity and the house mints no other.
* The compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` belongs to this slice's owner; cabal runtime work stops at its module boundary.

!!! 6 · TWO HEARTHS ~ the fresh start, then the ceremony (waits on everything above)
* The fresh start by hand (#/fresh-start) on both machines, on the re-found build.
* The ceremony: both found their seeds through the Stable act and stand; both found the Nexus fresh through the Nexus founding act; each signs a contract the two seats countersign; both join one shared cabal realm beside others; each wears a face; an edit crosses both ways and reads back after a restart on each machine. Probes use synthetic names; no fixture forges a cast persona's key or Handle.

!!! The sealed store ~ five steps threaded through the paths (operator-approved model; unbuilt)
* ''The model'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and in the operator's projected files alone. A private bag rests as authenticated ciphertext chunks, the same bytes at rest and in a blind carrier's hands; a node that holds the capability and stands locked rests sealed. Private bytes take randomized authenticated encryption addressed over the ciphertext, so convergent sealing leaves the private tier. A bag's content key rolls at a causal removal act, never by a clock.
* ''Step 1, after SUBSTRATE's seal'': the one private-byte format (chunk envelope, ciphertext address, key-version manifest, lockbox) and the amendments to `content-resolution.mem`, `blob-carriage.mem` and `cap-tier.mem`. The format changes ONCE, here. It also defines shareable-history epochs and authenticated checkpoint frontiers.
* ''Step 2'': durable, sealed bag lockboxes and a chunk index; the provider's tracked chunks rest in memory alone today.
* ''Step 3, after ONE FACE'': sealed storage values and hydration scoped by the session's face.
* ''Step 4, after the KAHU PLANE's seats and wire'': sealed history published to and fetched from a blind carrier by the house's own carriage protocol, with a causal index. A carrier never joins document sync.
* ''Step 5, after ONE FACE'': a public bag served at the floor of a locked node; today the floor serves the oracle snapshot alone.
* ''History at admission'' (operator ruling): sharing a bag's past with a newcomer must have stood declared when those edits got written. An inviter chooses between history the realm already marked shareable and a present-state admission, and never widens the audience for another writer's past. The design target keeps one logical bag name whose key rolls mark epochs: a removal rolls, a from-now admission rolls, and a with-its-past admission hands over the earlier epochs' keys.
* ''Mutual removal'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae">>): both signed acts and both key branches stay, every member sees the conflict, and it ends in an explicit fork unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.
* ''Owed first'': `foldEdgeKapae` projects no shadow for a same-version tie (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/edge-kapae.ts`), where kapae law keeps the shadow up.

!!! The shore ~ the browser's five rungs, beside the paths
* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
* ''What blocks rungs 0–2'': rungs 0 and 1 pass live (`tools/browser-ci.sh`, the crossing and render walks); rung 2's leaf half saves and survives a reload of the same profile, while its node half waits on ONE FACE's working layer. The node owes a ''durability receipt'' on the authenticated session (`docUrl`, the stored heads, the holder's boot epoch, sent only after the holder commits those heads; no clock in the frame).
* Device capabilities get measured by `tools/capability-probe/` on real devices, never assumed.
* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on #/open's second question before a persona root enters a browser.
* The walks ride `tools/browser-ci.sh`, and build under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`: a frame carries absolute state, and a worker defines no global `window`.

!!! Off this road, by ruling
* ''Cloud carriers'': capability and carrier law, private sealed carriage, per-store lifecycle, causal wake and its measurement. Deferred until the operator can deploy; the reading of "delegated keys" (a narrow carriage grant, or a backup that can act) stays open with them.
* ''The Sensorium'': waits behind the working-Lararium milestone.
* ''Streams'' (`saqimtiaz/streams`, BSD-3-Clause): a deferred crossroads offering; it never rides the genesis boot set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fresh-start>>

!! The fresh start ~ by hand, reversible, never a command

NO pave or wipe command stands (operator-ruled): such a command carries too much risk. In early alpha a fresh
START still reads pono when it runs BY HAND: MOVE the old homes aside — rename them, never delete them, so
the move stays reversible — then found fresh on the re-found build. Valuable data lives in git, in the
private `claude-backup` repository, or on `D:`; a vessel home holds nothing that only it holds.

A fresh machine needs no move: a fresh vessel seeds from the tracked `bags/` and the committed `genesis/`
(`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/fresh-state`).

The homes to move: the ones the OLD build's resolvers name — the `~/.lares` home, the XDG data homes for
`lares` and `lararium`, and the state home — asked of the old build at the moment of moving, never
enumerated by hand. A founding refuses a store that holds an earlier shape (`OldShapeRefusal`, before any
route gets asked and before any write), which keeps an accidental founding off any home left unmoved. Other
Nexuses rejoin through presented admits; every old admit names an old AID and reads dead by construction.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open>>

!! Open ~ what the operator still owns

# ''Seats and recovery.'' Single-seat Kahu acts, an operator's own herm, guardian count and succession, paper, the backup bundle, the catastrophe rite and a Handle's DNS binding stand open at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>.
# ''A root-holding browser leaf's backup slot.'' A WebCrypto non-extractable slot allows no paper backup in-browser; does "a backup at every tier" require such a leaf to hold a paper or passphrase slot too? Lean: yes for a leaf holding a persona root; a floor-only leaf holds no tree.
# ''A sealed receipt manifest for carriage.'' It would let a root restored from paper re-open its carriage after device loss — and would turn carriage toward the backup W rules it never becomes. The lenses split; re-opening W needs the operator's turned key.
# ''Carriage across leaves.'' A guest-held carry-claim proven at the carry door would let carriage follow a guest across leaves — a second relation and a second domain. Lean: not built.
# ''The fork-resolve act's shape.'' The seal record holds one predecessor.
# ''The re-seal proof.'' Name the causal record that maps old ciphertext to its replacement.

